  "_britishPhrases": "British PHRASES, and this list exists because the word list could not hold them. The reason vocabulary looked mostly unautomatable is that the list held single words, and most British words are also good American words with another meaning - so 'tick' cannot be banned when 'the next tick' and 'a ticket' are both fine. IN A PHRASE THE AMBIGUITY COLLAPSES: 'tick the box' has no American reading, and 'the next tick' does not match it. So a multi-word entry is decidable where a one-word entry is not, and this is where a British word that is also an American word belongs. WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN A MISSPELLING (Ken, September 10 2026): 'colour' for 'color' is merely wrong, and the reader knows exactly what is meant. 'Tick the box' when the screen shows a checkbox is MISLEADING - it names an action the user cannot find. A vocabulary error changes what the reader thinks they have to do. Matched case-insensitively with flexible whitespace; keep entries short and keep them to forms that could plausibly appear in this project's prose.",
